      "math_academy_id": "q-129182",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "For the function $f(x,y,z)=z-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1,$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "The level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ z-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1 & =1 \\\\ z & =4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=1$ is the elliptic paraboloid $z=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$",
